    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1286">

                <pb facs="1417" n="1405" xml:id="pg1405"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the Council of People’s Commissars of
                        the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stalin</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to President Roosevelt </hi><anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d1286-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p>I have received your message containing information regarding the issuance of
                    passports to Dr. Lange and priest Orlemansky. Although the Soviet transport is
                    considerably overloaded we shall provide Lange and Orlemansky with necessary
                    transport facilities. The Soviet Government is considering the trip of Lange and
                    Orlemansky to the Soviet Union as that of private citizens.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">[<placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi>
                        </placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-28">March 28,
                            1944</date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1286-f1" n="51" type="source" xml:id="d1286-f1-content">
                <p>Copy of telegram obtained from the Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, Hyde Park, N.Y.
                    Notations indicate that this message was received from Ambassador Gromyko at
                    4:15 p.m., and that a copy was sent to the Department of State on the 29th with
                    the President’s statement, “no reply necessary”.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
